  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThe forecast period begins Thursday with another day of dangerous \nheat as the main concern. Overall, expecting cooler temperatures \nlater in the day due to increasing cloud cover and rain chances. But\nahead of that temperatures could rise enough to result in a \nmoderate to major heat risk. A heat advisory will be in effect \nfrom 1 pm through approximately 7 pm. Please remember to take \nproper precautions to keep you and others safe from heat related \nillness. Attention then turns to Tropical Storm Bertha as the \nprimary forecast concern. Increasing tropical moisture will \nsupport greater rain chances across South Texas and the adjacent \ncoastal waters Thursday into Friday, followed by a transition \ntoward a more typical late-July pattern this weekend and next \nweek. Heat will remain an additional concern, particularly where \nrainfall is less widespread and during the increasingly dry period\nbehind Bertha. As of Wednesday afternoon, the center of Tropical \nStorm Bertha had moved inland near the St. Bernard Parish, \nLouisiana and Biloxi, MS areas after making landfall along the la \ncoast. Bertha has continued to move generally west along the \nnorthern Gulf Coast. Persistent northeasterly shear has displaced \nthe deepest convection south of the exposed low-level circulation,\nwhile dry-air entrainment and increasing interaction with land \nshould promote gradual weakening, the latest forecast continues to\nfavor Bertha weakening while tracking westward and eventually \ndissipating after moving inland over Eastern or Southeastern \nTexas. Some uncertainty remains regarding whether a better-defined\ncirculation could redevelop beneath the convection if Bertha \nspends additional time near or over the Gulf waters. Although the \ncenter of Bertha should remain well north of south texas, its \nbroad and asymmetric moisture will extend much farther south and \nwest than the circulation itself. Deep tropical moisture and weak \nlarge-scale ascent should spread into the region Thursday, with \nrain chances increasing from northeast to southwest during the \nday. Scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms Thursday \nafternoon and evening, with the greatest coverage across the \nVictoria Crossroads, Coastal Plains and Gulf waters. Locally heavy\nrainfall will be possible beneath the stronger and/or slower- \nmoving cells. Current forecasts continue to favor rainfall amounts\nof generally 1-3 inches with isolated amounts up to around 6 \ninches through Friday and the Weather Prediction Center (WPC) has \nmuch of South Texas south along the coast and up to the Coastal \nPlains and Victoria Crossroads under a marginal risk for excessive\nrainfall and flash flooding. There's a very low (~20%) chance for\ntropical storm force winds Thursday, primarily across the \nnortheastern counties of the northern Coastal Bend and Victoria \nCrossroads. Looking ahead to next week post Bertha, temperatures \nwill trend upward resulting in a return to moderate to major heat \nrisk.",
